              DIGITAL SELECTED TO MANAGE INFRASTRUCTURE
                      FOR THE MICROSOFT NETWORK


MAYNARD, Mass. -- November 14, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
has been awarded a five-year contract to manage the worldwide 
client/server infrastructure for The Microsoft Network.

     The contract includes the management of the wide-area backbone 
network, the data center, and the help desk function for the 
environment that will support Microsoft's products and services.

     Digital won the contract after a capabilities evaluation by 
Microsoft of other leading outsourcing firms.

     Under the terms of the outsourcing agreement, Digital will 
manage and operate a site consisting of more than 200 multivendor 
servers. The primary infrastructure operations will be set up in the 
Seattle (WA) area. Digital will also be enhancing its Polycenter 
management tools to support distributed Windows NT environments.

     "Microsoft's information technology strategy is built around a 
high performance Windows NT client/server infrastructure that 
employs hardware and software from a variety of vendors," noted Russ 
Siegelman, Microsoft's General Manager of Online Services. "We 
selected Digital because of their leadership in managing client/
server production environments."

     According to Bob Young, Digital's Managing Director for
Outsourcing Services, "this is a key win for Digital because it 
demonstrates our excellent competitive position in managing 
multivendor client/server networks. We're particularly pleased that 
such a well-respected leader in the computer industry has selected 
Digital to manage a critical element of its information technology 
infrastructure."

     Digital is noted for its modular approach to outsourcing, which 
provides clients with as much support as they need, in whatever form 
they choose in more than 100 countries around the world. This ranges 
from Digital providing advice and assistance to clients who manage 
their own operations, to shared management where Digital assumes 
responsibility for a specific information technology function or 
business process, to Digital assuming full outsourcing 
responsibility.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
