             DIGITAL REINFORCES LEADERSHIP IN WINDOWS NT
                     WITH POWERFUL NEW SOLUTIONS


    AlphaStation, AlphaServer, and System and Network Management
           Products Demonstrated at Digital's COMDEX Booth


LAS VEGAS (COMDEX) -- Nov. 14, 1994 --Digital Equipment Corporation 
is demonstrating several new Windows NT capabilities at COMDEX that 
further reinforce its position as the premier provider of Windows NT 
systems for the enterprise.

     Windows NT products and technologies being demonstrated include 
the operating system running on the company's powerful new 
AlphaStation and AlphaServer systems, and newly available, 
integrated system and network management platforms on Windows NT -- 
POLYCENTER AssetWORKS and POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows 
NT.  Digital will also have a technology demonstration of Digital 
Clusters for Windows NT, a key capability that will accelerate 
Windows NT adoption in the enterprise.

     "Windows NT is clearly a strategic platform for Digital, as our 
customers and partners increasingly demand products and services 
that support the operating system," said Enrico Pesatori, vice 
president and general manager, Computer Systems Group, at Digital.  
"Through our joint development efforts and partnership with 
Microsoft, Digital has become a leader in providing comprehensive, 
affordable and powerful Windows NT solutions on both Intel and RISC 
platforms."

Windows NT/AlphaStation and Alpha Servers

     Demonstrated at COMDEX will be Windows NT on Digital's next 
generation of AlphaStation and AlphaServer hardware platforms, 
announced by the company on November 3.  The AlphaStation product 
family combines the high performance and superior graphics typical 
of UNIX workstations with the compatibility and openness of PCs.  
The AlphaServer products feature large memory and storage capacity 
and industry-standard Peripheral Component Interconnect (PCI) to 
complement their scalable processor power.

     "The new AlphaStation and AlphaServer products provide 
customers increased choice and flexibility for Windows NT," said 
David Flawn, director of marketing, Windows NT at Digital.  "PC 
users benefit by having the same, consistent Windows interface they 
are accustomed to, on top of a powerful system for developing and 
integrating applications."

     There are 815 applications for Windows NT on Alpha being used 
today, and more than 2,100 committed for Alpha/Windows NT in the 
near future, Flawn added.

Integrated System and Network Management Platforms

     Also demonstrated in the show booth are the industry's first 
integrated multivendor system and network management platforms on 
Windows NT, announced today by Digital.

     POLYCENTER AssetWORKS, a Windows NT Workstation application 
based on Microsoft's System Management Server (SMS), makes it easy 
for customers to gain control of their hardware and software 
investments, and reduce operating costs. The application extends the 
inventory and software distribution and installation capabilities to 
multivendor UNIX systems -- SunOS, Solaris, AIX, HP-UX, DEC OSF/1, 
and ULTRIX -- and OpenVMS. AssetWORKS also enhances SMS by providing 
easy-to-use pre-defined and customizable reports, including system 
inventory, software by system, system by software, job status and 
many others.

     For open network management, Digital announced the availability 
of a software developers' kit for POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for 
Windows NT Workstation -- a port of the NetView network management 
platform jointly developed by Digital and IBM.  The product combines 
the ease-of-use of Windows 95 style with NetView's MOTIF graphical 
user interface to enhance its usability.

     "Our major customers are embracing Windows NT and need 
industrial strength management applications.  This announcement is 
our first step in delivering comprehensive management solutions for 
Windows NT," said Nancy Strecker, vice president, Digital Software 
Products.  "These exciting new POLYCENTER products demonstrate 
Digital's continued leadership in managing open client/server 
environments."

     POLYCENTER AssetWORKS will be available in December 1994 on 
both Intel and Alpha processors.  An AssetWORKS and SMS server 
packaged together is priced at $1500.  Agent prices vary from $25 
to 250 depending on the number of agents purchased and the operating 
systems supported.  The software developer's kit for POLYCENTER 
Manager on NetView for Windows NT Workstation will be available in 
December at no cost through the NetView Association, which recently 
expanded its support to include Windows NT.

Clusters for Windows NT

     Digital will also have a technology demonstration of its 
Clusters for Windows NT product.  Clusters, a technology solution 
pioneered by Digital, are loosely coupled sets of systems that 
behave like a single system while providing high levels of 
availability through redundant CPUs, storage and data paths.  
Clusters for Windows NT is being designed from the ground up to 
bring the benefits of distributed availability and scalability to 
client/server workloads and usage models.  

     "Companies deploying client/server solutions are concerned 
about system reliability and cost-effective growth paths," said 
Flawn.  "Clusters for Windows NT will address these critical 
concerns by enhancing the availability, scalability, and management 
of data and key services within a client/server LAN environment."

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
