       DIGITAL TO PROVIDE INTERNET TOOLS ON CD-ROM FOR WINDOWS NT


Seattle, WA -- October 27, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation
today announced a new CDROM-based subscription that it will begin
shipping in November with its packaged Windows NT (TM) systems. Called
The Road Map, the free CD includes an up-to-date selection of Internet
and software development tools, as well as product and services
information and application catalogs. It uses mosaic, the widely
popular browser for the Internet, as its interface and provides a highly
interactive, automated, resource for customers of Digital's NT products
and services.

     "The Internet holds a vast repository of useful information and
tools, but users find that the frustrations involved in locating and
qualifying resources impede adoption," said Edmund Cuoco, Digital's
Business Development Manager for Windows NT systems. "We have done the
up-front work of qualifying products for our platforms and where
possible added value by including Digital-developed tools and
documentation not destined for commercial products but widely used by
our internal applications groups. We have also invested time in
carefully selecting the best shareware and public domain implementations
when multiple sources exist."

     "With The Road Map, Digital has continued its tradition of using
the Internet and related technologies to deliver innovative customer
solutions," said Douglas Colbeth, President of Spyglass, Inc. "In this
case, they are using the same browser and associated protocols that have
fueled the explosive growth on the Internet to provide a very useful
resource to its customers. You could almost call it 'a Worldwide Web on
CD'. It will be an invaluable tool for those developing Internet
storefronts or internal networked information systems."

     The Road Map contains a wide assortment of tools and information
for Digital's Intel (TM) and Alpha (TM) systems packaged with Microsoft
(TM) Windows NT. Internet resources include a mosaic browser and
associated graphic viewers and multimedia players, Web servers and
authoring tools, and WAIS servers. Wherever possible, development
toolkits and documentation are supplied. Software development tools
include editors, archivers, connectivity services, optimizers, migration
tools and guides, and management tools. There are even several desktop
applications and popular games. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide
information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP platforms, storage,
networking, software and services, together with industry-focused
solutions from business partners, help organizations compete and win in
today's global marketplace.
