               DIGITAL SHOWCASES PERFORMANCE SOLUTIONS
             TO MEET THE WORLDS MOST DEMANDING COMPUTING
           REQUIREMENTS AT SUPERCOMPUTING '94 IN BOOTH 202


      ... Digital demonstrates world's fastest microprocessor,
                 highest performance Alpha System...


WASHINGTON, DC -- November 14, 1994 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation's technical computing prowess is clearly visible at the 
Supercomputing '94 Conference and Exhibition, held this week in the 
Washington Convention Center. In Digital's booth, #202, conference 
attendees can experience, firsthand, the breadth of Digital's 
solutions to address the computing needs of engineers, scientists 
and researchers. 

     "While other vendors talk about delivering high performance
microprocessors, Digital is doing it," said John O'Keefe, vice
president, UNIX Systems Marketing. "Digital's display ranges from 
the 'Power Desktop' Alpha workstations to high performance scalable
parallel processing with SMP and cluster systems based on 64-bit 
Alpha technology - fastest in the world and here today." 

     "We are demonstrating the first system implementation of the 
300 MHz Alpha chip," added O'Keefe. "This is a 4-processor server 
running Digital's standard UNIX operating system which can scale to 
twelve processors in the same cabinet. It is just one example of 
Digital's commitment to empower customers with products which offer 
them a clear the competitive advantage." 

Also featured in the Digital booth are: 

     o  An Integrated Design and Analysis demonstration featuring a 
        16 processor AdvantageCluster Compute Server 5000 
        configuration which dramatically illustrates the increased 
        throughput possible from running concurrent jobs on multiple 
        processors. 

     o  Collaborative computing with Communique! from InSoft 
        Corporation, PROengineer from Parametric Technology, and 
        Mechanica from RASNA. This demonstration shows the power and 
        efficiency of design and analysis applications used by 
        development groups in geographically dispersed 
        organizations. 

     o  Digital's Velocitor High Performance IPI Disk Storage which 
        further extends the industry leading performance of Alpha 
        workstations. 

     o  A Virtual Reality area featuring an exciting look at an
     	anti-terrorist tactics simulation played out in Cyberspace. 

     o  NEW TODAY -- The AdvantageCluster Compute Server 5000 system
     	offering performance of over 3 GIGAFLOPS for just over 
        $320K, a new record in price/performance for clustered 
        systems. 

     o  Digital's first-in-the-world general purpose High 
        Performance Fortran-90 implementation, which simplifies 
        customers' development and deployment of parallel 
        application code. The demonstration also features Digital's 
        Parallel Software Environment. 

     o  A demonstration of Digital's unique ATM Networked UNIX 
        Cluster.  Digital in cooperation with Sandia National Labs 
        and Lawrence Livermore National Labs will show in all 3 
        booths a distributed UNIX cluster connected by high speed 
        ATM switches. Providing 50% higher bandwidth than the award 
        winning FDDI GIGAswitch, ATM is the high performance inter-
     	system interconnect of the future available from Digital 
        today. 

     o  A display of Digital's implementation of the MEMORY CHANNEL
     	interconnect which dramatically demonstrates how Digital can 
        extend the features, functionality and performance of its 
        AdvantageCluster family of systems and deliver a low-cost 
        alternative to traditional supercomputers. 

     Over a dozen Alpha workstations are distributed throughout the
Supercomputing '94 exhibit area in software and research partners'
exhibits. Researchers include Vanderbilt University, Lawrence
Livermore National Labs, Ohio State University, Oregon State
University, Oak Ridge National Lab, Purdue University, NCSA, Temple
University, and Georgia Tech. 

     On the second day of the conference, Wednesday November 16, 
Rick Gillett, Digital senior consulting engineer, will present a 
paper,  "Parallel Scalable Computing - The Next Generation" as part 
of the Exhibitor Forum series. The presentation is scheduled for 
10:15 AM and covers Digital's approach to delivering highly 
available parallel computing with UNIX clusters and MEMORY CHANNEL. 

     Digital is also sponsoring the Internet E-mail room for all
Supercomputing '94 attendees. Digital is a leader in supplying a
