       DIGITAL EQUIPMENT VIDEO SERVER TECHNOLOGY SELECTED FOR
               EUROPEAN CABLE TV VIDEO-ON-DEMAND TRIAL


MAYNARD, Mass. -- November 22, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that its video server technology is being used in 
one of Europe's first trials of video-on-demand services conducted 
by a cable TV operator.  The trial, to take place in a suburb south 
of Stockholm, Sweden, is being undertaken by Svenska Kabel-TV and 
Telia AB, the Swedish national phone service provider.

     The market test will be conducted with 500 households in 
Jarlaberg, starting in early 1995.  Households selected for the 
trial will be able to order from a selection of 40 movies via 
telephone and a customized text-TV system.  Under terms of the 
cooperative agreement, Digital is providing its server technology at 
Svenska Kabel-TV's head-end operation, as well as its advanced 
middleware platform, which integrates a number of software layers 
that provide complete video-on-demand service, including order and 
billing systems.

     Hans Brattberg, technical director of Svenska Kabel-TV, stated, 
"Digital's end-to-end architecture approach, combined with its 
systems integration expertise, really differentiates them from other 
video server providers.  In addition, their non-proprietary, 
standards-based approach contributes to the cost-effective delivery 
of interactive video services."

     According to Sultan Zia, general manager of Digital's Video and 
Interactive Information Services, "As one of the first cable TV 
operators to invest in digital infrastructure, Svenska Kabel-TV is 
at the forefront of bringing interactive media to cable subscribers.  
By choosing Digital's solution, they are well-positioned to easily 
expand their service offerings incrementally and enhance their 
market leadership position."

     Vela Research, Inc., is providing the MPEG-2 decoder cards that 
convert the video servers' digital signals into analog form for 
transmission over the cable company's existing network. (more)

     Digital Equipment Video Servers Selected for First European 
Video-on-Demand Trial/2 Digital's Video and Interactive Information 
Architecture harnesses the power of Alpha, the world's fastest 
64-bit processor, combined with StorageWorks disk arrays and Digital 
Linear Tape library systems.  The architecture, designed for 
cost-effective implementation, includes a comprehensive set of 
standards-based network media server middleware that enables service 
providers to easily integrate a range of interactive media 
applications with existing networks, PCs, and a variety of set-top 
boxes.

     Digital's media server has already been selected for more 
market trials and deployments than any other server vendor, 
including U S West's trial in Nebraska; Nynex deployments in Rhode 
Island, Massachusetts, and Maine; and Telstra's first pilot program 
