                    DIGITAL UNVEILS LOTUS AND SUN
                     CONNECTIVITY WITH MAILWORKS


      ...new products transform PC LAN mail to mission-critical
                     client/server messaging...


  ...world's first X.500 directory to meet U.S. GOSIP standards...


BOSTON, Mass. -- November 29, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced at EMAIL WORLD a new version of its MailWorks 
electronic messaging software that provides enterprise messaging 
capabilities to Lotus cc:Mail and Sun Microsystems users. Digital 
also announced today that its X.500 directory product, which can be 
accessed by MailWorks users, is the first in the world to be 
registered as U.S. GOSIP (Government Open Systems Interconnect 
Procurement) conformant and to complete the test suite of the 
European-based OSTC (Open Systems Testing Consortium). Also 
announced today are new factory-installed, ready-to-run 
configurations of the new AlphaServer systems announced earlier this 
month.

     Digital's MailWorks client/server software for LAN mail 
connectivity combines the reliability and security of host-based 
systems with the flexibility and ease-of-use of PC LAN mail systems. 
The cc:Mail Connection for MailWorks Version 1.0 product, developed 
in cooperation with Lotus Development Corporation, transforms the 
environment of the cc:Mail for Windows Version 2.0 user from PC LAN 
file sharing technology into powerful client/server enterprise 
messaging. The cc:Mail Connection for MailWorks eliminates the need for 
multiple post offices, gateways, routers, and directory 
synchronization and offers faster message delivery and lower cost of 
ownership than LAN based file sharing mail systems. The conversion 
to the MailWorks server software is transparent to users, who 
continue to use cc:Mail with no change. Additional menu options are 
available to access services provided by MailWorks such as secure, 
server-based storage and the DEC X.500 Directory Service software.

     With the new MailWorks Client for SunOS software, Sun 
workstation users can now connect to a MailWorks server which 
provides access to an enterprise wide messaging environment. Sun 
users can now access X.500 directories, send and receive tagged 
enclosures, and connect to both SMTP (Simple Mail Transport 
Protocol) and X.400 mail networks without the use of gateways. In 
addition to SunOS, the MailWorks server supports a wide range of 
client platforms including Macintosh, Microsoft Windows, DOS, UNIX 
workstations, and video terminals.

     The new version of Digital's MailWorks for OSF/1 server 
software, Version 1.5, includes new features that reduce the cost of 
ownership for managing LAN mail environments. A facility has been 
added to MailWorks which guides administrators through a simple 
procedure to add or update user account information. In addition, a 
network monitoring tool will be available early next year to provide 
a graphical view of the status of the mail network.

     With the introduction of the new MailWorks version, new 
concurrent-user license options are available. Licenses may be 
ordered in increments of 1-user, 25-users, 50-users, and 100-users. 
The increments are individually priced to make these options 
attractive for configurations of hundreds of users as well as small 
workgroups; increments can be easily added as workgroups grow in 
size. For organizations that already use cc:Mail or Microsoft Mail, 
the MailWorks client/server upgrade is attractively priced.

World's first GOSIP conformant X.500

     MailWorks software includes a local directory with a capacity 
of 5,000 users. Organizations with requirements for higher 
capacities or that wish to use an industry standard directory may 
incorporate the DEC X.500 Directory Service product, which is 
implemented according to the 1993 International Telecommunications 
Union (formerly CCITT) X.500 recommendations. This month the product 
has successfully completed the OSTC tests and the results have been 
accepted by the National Institute for Standards and Technology to 
register Digital's directory software as the world's first X.500 
product to meet U.S. GOSIP standards.

New factory-installed packages ready to run in less than an hour

     AlphaServer models can now be ordered with a factory-installed 
MailWorks package for easy start-up. Administrators can set up a 
functional system in less than an hour by following a four-step 
process: uncrate, power up, enter a network node name and address, 
and enter users. A license which can typically support 75-100 users 
(license limited to 25 concurrent users) is included with the 
ready-to-run package. The AlphaServer packages are designed for use 
by departments and remote offices and may be used as building blocks 
for expanding networks.

     MailWorks for OSF/1 Version 1.5 software is available in 
December; pricing ranges from $90 for a 1-user concurrent license to 
$4,000 for an unlimited capacity license. The cc:Mail Connection for 
MailWorks Version 1.0 product will be available in February; the 
media kit is priced at $29.00 and the unlimited user license is 
included with the MailWorks product. The MailWorks Client for SunOS 
product will be available in December and is priced at $140 per 
user. An AlphaServer system with factory-installed MailWorks 
software to support 500 users will be available in December for less 
than $75 per user. DEC X.500 Directory Service Version 1.3 is 
currently available and capacity pricing ranges from $5,000 to 
$20,000.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
