            DIGITAL PRESIDENT AND CEO POINTS TO PROGRESS
                IN RETURNING COMPANY TO PROFITABILITY


BOSTON, Mass. -- November 10, 1994  -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
is seeing "significant and encouraging signs" in its efforts to 
return to profitability, President and Chief Executive Officer 
Robert B. Palmer said today at Digital's annual stockholders' 
meeting.

     Detailing the company's mission to be a leader in helping 
customers implement open client/server computing environments, 
Palmer said he is confident that Digital has made "the right 
adjustments, the right organizational decisions and is on the right 
track."

     Digital is a "leaner, more decisive, more agile company -- 
sharply focused on meeting customer needs, the realities of the 
marketplace and the challenges of our competitors," he said.

     Looking forward, Palmer said Digital will enter calendar year 
1995 with the strongest products it has ever had, with an entire new 
line of personal computers including a new line of notebook personal 
computers that will be unveiled in New York City this Friday.  In 
addition, the company recently introduced an entire new line of 
64-bit RISC AlphaStation and AlphaServer systems.

     "Fundamentally, we have completely refreshed our hardware 
product line -- a strong position to be in as we move into the new 
calendar year," he said.

     Palmer reiterated his pledge that Digital will provide the 
choice and flexibility customers want by offering three operating 
systems for Alpha platform customers:  OpenVMS, DEC OSF/1 (Digital's 
UNIX) and Microsoft's Windows NT, and by supporting applications 
provided by partners and third parties.  Those offerings are 
complemented by Digital's outstanding customer service and support, 
layered software products and components. 

     "Digital's key value to customers - our differentiation in the 
marketplace -- is this:  We have the ability - directly and through 
partners - to implement and support networked platforms and 
applications in today's multivendor open computing environments more 
quickly and more cost-effectively than anyone else," he said.

     "To deliver on this commitment," Palmer said, "we are depending 
on our world-class systems integration capability -- consistently 
ranked in the top five of worldwide integrators -- and our 
outstanding network integration capability as well, supported at 
every level by our multivendor customer service organization, which 
is already at benchmark level for the industry."

     Palmer said the company is successfully implementing its 
strategy of increasing sales through indirect channels.  In the 
first quarter of fiscal 1995, Digital generated 57 percent of its 
product revenue through indirect channels -- close to the company 
goal of more than 60 percent for this fiscal year.

     "We still have work to do and we still must make additional 
improvements to our business, but we have the technology, the 
products and services, the financial resources, the talent and the 
resolve necessary to ensure Digital's future as a preferred 
strategic supplier of information technology -- and as a leader in 
today's open client/server computing environments," Palmer said.

In annual meeting business, stockholders approved:

     o  The election of Vernon R. Alden, Robert R. Everett, Thomas 
        L. Phillips and Delbert C. Staley to three-year terms on the 
        Board of Directors;

     o  An amendment to the 1968 Employee Stock Purchase Plan to 
        increase the number of shares available for issuance by 5 
        million shares;

     o  An amendment to the 1981 International Employee Stock 
        Purchase Plan to increase the number of shares available for 
        issuance by 3.5 million shares; and

     o  The selection of the firm of Coopers & Lybrand L.L.P as 
        auditors for the fiscal year ending July 1, 1995.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.				    
