      LARGEST LIVE INTERNET PROJECT PROVIDES ELECTION RESULTS:
                 OVER A MILLION ACCESSES MADE TO THE
                     CALIFORNIA ELECTION SYSTEM


         Digital and State of California Continue to Provide
                      Post-Election Information


SACRAMENTO, Calif. -- November 10, 1994 -- California Acting 
Secretary of State Tony Miller and Digital Equipment Corporation today
declared the Internet California election project an enormous success. 
Over a million accesses have been made since the beginning of election
day, making this the largest Internet project undertaken. Users,
including TV stations receiving live election result feed, were able to
get immediate information on the many down-to-the-wire races and ballot
issues, some of which were not determined in time for coverage in
November 9 morning newspapers. A vast number of users continue to
retrieve post-election information, including summary of data and
voting results.

[To reach the Digital/State of California World-Wide Web server for 
post-election summary of data and voting results, type either 
http://www.election.digital.com or http://www.election.ca.gov. To  
reach the Gopher server, for textual information and flat statistics, 
type gopher.election.ca.gov.]

     According to Miller, "We're excited the joint efforts between 
Digital and my office have provided Californians the most up-to-date 
information on candidates and ballot propositions of major concern.  
The number of accesses to the live election returns and the post-
election summaries, shows Californians have a strong interest in 
using technology in the democratic process.  The State will be 
looking at ways to continue providing this service to our citizens 
in the future."

     "Californians have once again shown leadership in using 
cutting-edge technology and we are honored to be the company helping 
the State realize its goals of making critical information available 
to the public," said Rose Ann Giordano, vice president of Digital's 
Internet Business Group.  "By supplying a full range of services and 
products to facilitate the Internet/California State Election 
system, we're delivering on our commitment to make today's Internet 
a secure, viable communications vehicle for public and commercial 
interests."

     Digital's history of Internet leadership, including investing 
in significant Internet research efforts, provided the knowledge 
needed to design and deliver, in only four weeks, the robust, 
interactive solution for accessing dynamic on-line information. The 
company's experiences were gained by being the first Fortune 500 
company to put a multimedia server on the World-Wide Web, the first
computer vendor to enable customers to test-drive products over the 
Internet, and the first to install an interactive "village," e.g., 
the Citizens' Network for the City of Palo Alto.

Internet/California Election System Content

     California Secretary of State staff and members of Digital's 
Internet Business Group and Digital's California-based research 
labs, together delivered the California Election/Internet system.  
In addition to live, on-line election night returns, a wealth of 
information continues to be available on the Web server:

     o	Final election results

     o	Interactive map of California counties, which provides 
        return information by county

     o	Access statistics and general feedback from assessors.  
        (Note:  the feedback will not be attributed to any 
        individual.)

     o	Entire contents of the official California Voter Information 
        Pamphlet published by the Secretary of State's office, e.g., 
        state-wide candidates, ballot propositions (full text and 
        nonpartisan analysis), political party goals, and electoral 
        procedures

